      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Norwegian immigrant Ole Colbjornson Livdahlen arrived in July 1853 and built a log house near present‑day Gordonsville, marking Glenville’s first European settlement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/glenville_minnesota",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Glenville’s origins trace back to the mid‑1850s, when early settlers followed federal land surveys and the broader westward expansion.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/glenville_minnesota",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Norwegian and German immigrants flocked to Glenville in the 1860s, drawn by the county’s loamy soils ideal for farming.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/glenville_minnesota",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Glenville sits along the Shell Rock River just south of Albert Lea, giving the town a scenic waterway backdrop.",
